======================================================================
PART 1: TOP EIGENVALUE OF C_T^TC_T
======================================================================
n=6: Top eigenvalue of C_T^TC_T across 500 random tournaments:
  Min top eig: 6.000000
  Max top eig: 6.000000
  ALL = 6? True
  t₃ range: [12, 20]

PROOF that top eigenvalue = n for ALL tournaments with t₃ > 0:

Let C be the FULL constraint matrix (transitive tournament, C(n,3) rows).
C^TC = n·P where P is projection onto rank-(n-1)(n-2)/2 subspace (THM-224).

For tournament T, C_T is C restricted to its t₃ transitive triple rows.
Let C_R be the REMOVED rows (cyclic triples). Then:
  C^TC = C_T^TC_T + C_R^TC_R

So C_T^TC_T = n·P - C_R^TC_R, and eigenvalues of C_T^TC_T ≤ n.

Top eigenvalue = n iff ∃ v with C_T^TC_T·v = n·v, i.e., C_R^TC_R·v = 0.
This means v ⊥ all rows of C_R, i.e., v is consistent with 
the REMOVED constraints (even though they're "wrong" for this tournament).

CLAIM: Such v always exists when t₃ ≥ n.

The rows of C_R span a subspace of dimension ≤ |C_R| = C(n,3) - t₃.
We need v ∈ im(C^T) ∩ ker(C_R), so:
  dim(im(C^T) ∩ ker(C_R)) ≥ dim(im(C^T)) + dim(ker(C_R)) - E
  = (n-1)(n-2)/2 + (E - rank(C_R)) - E
  = (n-1)(n-2)/2 - rank(C_R)
  ≥ (n-1)(n-2)/2 - (C(n,3) - t₃)

This is > 0 when t₃ > C(n,3) - (n-1)(n-2)/2 = C(n-1,3) = redundancy.

For n=5: C(n-1,3) = C(4,3) = 4. So t₃ > 4, i.e., t₃ ≥ 5. ✓ (min t₃=5)
For n=6: C(n-1,3) = C(5,3) = 10. So t₃ > 10, i.e., t₃ ≥ 11.
  But min t₃ at n=6 can be less than 11! Let me check...

Minimum t₃ at n=6 (sampled 2000): 12
C(n-1,3) = 10
Need t₃ > 10 for guaranteed top eig = n
  New min t₃=12, top eig=6.0000, bits=29452

Best min t₃ found at n=6: 12

======================================================================
PART 2: THE SPECTRAL TRANSITION AT t₃ = max_rank
======================================================================

At n=5, max_rank = 6. The data shows:

  t₃ < 6 (=5): rank=5, β₁=1 ALWAYS. Not enough constraints.
  t₃ = 6:      rank=5 or 6. DEPENDS ON WHICH triples.
  t₃ > 6:      rank=6 MORE LIKELY. Redundancy helps.
  t₃ ≥ 8:      rank=6 ALWAYS.

The transition at t₃ = max_rank is the CRITICAL POINT.
At this point, independence ratio = max_rank/t₃ = 1.

Below: independence ratio > 1 (system is "overloaded" — would need 
  more independent constraints than exist).
Above: independence ratio < 1 (system has redundancy).

The transitive tournament has ratio = 3/n (maximum redundancy).


======================================================================
PART 3: THM-217 AT x = n²·REDUNDANCY
======================================================================

THM-217 transfer matrix M(x) = [[1,2x,0],[0,0,1],[1,x,0]]
Char poly: λ³ - λ² - xλ - x = 0

At x = n(n-3) = n²·(redundancy fraction):
  λ³ - λ² - n(n-3)λ - n(n-3) = 0

At n=6: λ³ - λ² - 18λ - 18 = (λ+3)(λ² - 4λ - 6) = 0
  Eigenvalues: -3, 2+√10, 2-√10

MEANING of the eigenvalues:
  α = 3-n = -n·redundancy_fraction
    → This eigenvalue DIRECTLY encodes the constraint redundancy.
    → At n=6: α = -3 = -6·(1/2) = -n/2.
    → The redundancy eigenvalue governs ALTERNATING behavior.

  λ₁ = (n-2+√(n²+4))/2 = metallic mean M_n - 1
    → Growth rate of the recurrence a_{k+1} = (n-2)a_k + n·a_{k-1}
    → The "propagation" eigenvalue.

  λ₂ = -n/λ₁ = (n-2-√(n²+4))/2
    → The "correction" eigenvalue. |λ₂| → 1 as n → ∞.


